<article class="env-lemma" id="0FAK">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0FAK">Lemma <span data-tag="0FAK">42.47.7</span>.</a> In Lemma <a href="/tag/0F9H" data-tag="0F9H">42.47.1</a> assume $E_2|_{X_1 \cap X_2}$ is zero. Then </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \begin{align*}  P'_1(E_2) &  = c'_1(E_2), \\  P'_2(E_2) &  = c'_1(E_2)^2 - 2c'_2(E_2), \\  P'_3(E_2) &  = c'_1(E_2)^3 - 3c'_1(E_2)c'_2(E_2) + 3c'_3(E_2), \\  P'_4(E_2) &  = c'_1(E_2)^4 - 4c'_1(E_2)^2c'_2(E_2) + 4c'_1(E_2)c'_3(E_2) + 2c'_2(E_2)^2 - 4c'_4(E_2), \end{align*}
</div>
  <p> and so on with multiplication as in Remark <a href="/tag/0FA0" data-tag="0FA0">42.34.7</a>. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      The statement makes sense because the zero sheaf has rank $< 1$ and hence the classes $c'_ p(E_2)$ are defined for all $p \geq 1$. The equalities follow immediately from the characterization of the classes produced by Lemma <a href="/tag/0F9H" data-tag="0F9H">42.47.1</a> and the corresponding result for capping with the Chern classes of $E_2$ given in Remark <a href="/tag/0FAD" data-tag="0FAD">42.46.8</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>